---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { site, reviewCriteria, promises } from '../data/site';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Editorial Policy: How We Review | CheckVitalsNow"
  description="How CheckVitalsNow researches, scores, and updates supplement reviews: our sources, our five rating criteria, and the rules we never break."
  h1="Editorial Policy: How We Review Supplements"
>
  <p>
    Supplement marketing is loud, and many "reviews" online are sales pages in disguise. This page explains exactly how
    we work, so you can judge our reviews for yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>What our reviews are based on</h2>
  <p>
    Every review is based on the official formula, the ingredient research, the manufacturer's policies, and publicly
    available customer feedback. In practice, that means we check:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The product label and the manufacturer's official website.</li>
    <li>
      Published research on the key ingredients, preferably from primary sources such as PubMed, the National Institutes
      of Health (NIH), and peer-reviewed journals.
    </li>
    <li>The price, the refund or money-back terms, and where the product is officially sold.</li>
    <li>Public customer feedback, both positive and negative.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We do not run lab tests or clinical trials, and we do not claim personal results we did not have. When we have not
    been able to confirm a detail, such as an ingredient amount, we say so.
  </p>

  <h2>How we score products</h2>
  <p>Product reviews score each supplement from 0 to 10 on the same five criteria, so one review can be compared with another:</p>
  <ol>
    {
      reviewCriteria.map((c) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{c.name}:</strong> {c.text}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>
  <p>
    Comparison lists, such as our <a href="/best/bladder-supplements-for-women/">best bladder supplements for women</a>,
    use criteria and weights that fit the question the list answers. Those criteria and weights are always published on
    the list itself, and the #1 pick has to be defensible by them.
  </p>

  <h2>Health claims and safety</h2>
  <p>
    Dietary supplements are not approved by the FDA to diagnose, treat, cure, or prevent any disease, and we never say
    they do. We describe what manufacturers claim as their claims, report what the research does and does not show, and
    include a safety section that says who should talk to a doctor first. Every health guide includes the warning signs
    that mean you should see a doctor.
  </p>

  <h2>Our rules</h2>
  <ul>
    {promises.map((p) => <li>{p}</li>)}
    <li>
      If we show customer testimonials, they come from the manufacturer's own materials and are clearly labeled as such.
      We cannot verify them, and we say so.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Affiliate links and independence</h2>
  <p>
    {site.name} earns commissions from some links. Commissions never decide a product's score, and products without an
    affiliate link can appear in our lists when they deserve to. See our <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate
    disclosure</a> for details.
  </p>

  <h2>Updates and corrections</h2>
  <p>
    Formulas, prices, and guarantees change. Each page shows when it was last updated, and we revise pages when we learn
    that something has changed. If you find an error, please tell us through our <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>,
    and we will check it and correct it.
  </p>
</PageLayout>
